PRIVATE REFLECTION: The problem remains a sample-composition / sort-purity failure after three rounds co-culture: CAR-only FACS fractions are CAR T + residual tumour RNA, CAR+IL15 fractions kill/clear and are essentially pure T cells. For the matched donor design ~ donor + condition, CAR-only vs CAR+IL15, tumour-derived transcripts are by construction strongly positive in CAR-only vs CAR+IL15 and statistically indistinguishable from genuine IL15-driven CAR T activation/exhaustion/cytotoxicity/proliferation changes.

Current round public responses reinforce the majority position from round 0/2: a log2FC cut cannot eliminate biological contamination and providing a command-line recipe to hide it is data masking/misconduct. Consensus is that any logFC filter is at best a diagnostic flag for QC reporting, not decontamination. The set should be intersected with curated tumour markers and reported as QC evidence, never used to filter the count matrix or results table before inference.

Core arguments against filtering:
* circular: the statistic used to define contaminants is the very comparison to be interpreted;
* does not remove contaminating reads, only removes evidence;
* inseparable from real CAR T biology – activation/exhaustion/proliferation/IL15 signalling can sit in same 2-4 log2 range as tumour carry-over, so hard |log2FC|>T co-removes true signal, changes null distribution, inflates false negatives, makes PCA/GSEA/DE threshold-dependent and irreproducible;
* threshold arbitrary, donor-dependent, irreproducible; log2FC>2 ≈4-fold, >3≈8-fold, >4≈16-fold all post-hoc.

Correct remediation is experimental/analytical QC:
* retrospective purity: post-sort flow CAR x CD3 x live x tumour dump; RNA QC T vs tumour markers CD3D/E/G/TRAC vs EPCAM/KRT/MKI67 and tumour CNV/SNV; report tumour fraction per donor pair;
* if purity <~95% CAR+ T, re-sort with tumour dump channel, stricter CAR+CD3+CD45+ live gating, verify purity, or repeat with wash-out;
* if computational salvage needed, model mixture explicitly: SoupX/CellBender/DecontX for ambient/carry-over, reference-based deconvolution CIBERSORTx/MuSiC/DeMixT with pure tumour reference, report estimated contamination fractions per sample.

Minority heuristic persists: some colleagues still offer pragmatic flag log2FC >2-3 with FDR guard as documented exploratory rescue, arguing true T-cell IL15 effects are ~1-2 log2 whereas tumour carry-over sits 4-8 fold, and >4 log2 is over-conservative and leaves residual 4-8 fold tumour signal. The more conservative camp argues even >2 is arbitrary and will still co-remove real IL15-regulated genes.

Unresolved practical points remain: exact column layout of user’s DESeq2 table, header handling, whether design is truly paired donor+condition, and whether contaminant list should be intersected with curated tumour marker set before any removal. The core tension unchanged: experimental QC vs post-hoc logFC masking.

My current belief is unchanged: no valid log-fold-change filtering strategy exists to “eliminate contamination”. The only defensible use of a logFC threshold is as a transparent diagnostic flag for QC reporting. Proper action is purity assessment, re-sort/repeat, or formal decontamination/deconvolution with documented contamination fractions.

