Minority heuristic persists in round 3: colleagues 13,14,16,17,21 argue pragmatic flag log2FC >2-3 with FDR support as documented exploratory rescue, claiming true T-cell IL15 effects are ~1-2 log2 whereas tumour carry-over after three rounds with washes is diluted to 4-8 fold, i.e. log2FC ~2-3, and >4 log2 is over-conservative and leaves residual 4-8 fold tumour signal. Conservative camp counters that even >2 is arbitrary and will still co-remove real IL15-regulated genes and is post-hoc cherry picking.

Unresolved practical points remain: exact column layout of user's DESeq2 table, header handling, whether design is truly paired donor+condition, and whether contaminant list should be intersected with curated tumour marker set before any removal.

My current belief is unchanged: no valid log-fold-change filtering strategy exists to “eliminate contamination”. The only defensible use of a logFC threshold is as a transparent diagnostic flag for QC reporting. Proper action is purity assessment, re-sort/repeat, or formal decontamination/deconvolution with documented contamination fractions.

